          "source_quote": "ἡμᾶς ἀγαπῶν ἀκριτὶ αὐτὸς μὲν ἐτελεύτησας",
          "english_quote": "Loving us, you yourself died without judgment",
          "issue": "The main text attaches ἀκριτὶ to dying. That is a defensible choice, but the note lists lexical alternatives without preserving the material alternative attachment to ἡμᾶς ἀγαπῶν: loving without discrimination or deliberation. “Without judgment” does not preserve that attachment once “Loving us” has been separated by a comma. The note also does not explain why the death attachment was preferred.",
          "suggested_revision": "Expand the note to distinguish the two attachments explicitly. If retaining the present main text, explain why ἀκριτὶ is taken with ἐτελεύτησας and clarify that a hearing, tribunal, or unresolved legal proceeding is not asserted by the Greek. Preserve the alternative “loving us without discrimination/deliberation.” This disclosure issue does not make the construction untranslatable.",
          "severity": "minor"
        },
